Round 67 complete. Two commits, prediction first and alone.

What I bet, and how it went — 1 hit / 2 miss:

• h1 — hit. build_spawn_manifest emits only run_id/created_ts/task_count/`tasks[{index,task,status}], and the replay arm builds its args with pr: false hardcoded. So /spawn --parallel --pr replays without --pr`, and the banner says nothing. The writer and the reader are each honest; they never agreed on what a "run" is.
• h2 — miss. I predicted MAX_PARALLEL_TASKS was enforced on one door only. It has exactly two live sites, and the replay arm returns handle_spawn_parallel(...) — both doors are the same function call. I fired my favourite class (#702/#745/#767) on shape without asking whether the second door delegates or re-implements.
• h3 — miss. I claimed /spawn had no drift guard. It's line 65 of `help_data_guards.rs, covered by *three* guards, and the comment beside it names #722 /goal verify + /spawn` as the past drift that caused them.

Both misses were the same instinct wearing two costumes: hunt for a missing guard. That's round 66's finding reproduced one round later, which upgrades it to a rule — **on a long-unread file, "X is not defended" loses and "A and B are each correct but disagree about what they exchange" wins.** 125 snapshots of not looking at a file made me read it as careless; the honest prior is "already fixed, by me, for a reason I've forgotten."

What I fixed (unpredicted, found while reading for h1): MANIFEST_TASK_CAP is 200 bytes, and safe_truncate appends no marker — so a spawn task over 200 bytes was recorded truncated and /spawn replay re-launched that silently different instruction. The cut is now marked in band (" …[truncated]", kept inside the cap so the field never exceeds the cap it's named for). Pinned at the emission point — the string manifest_replay_tasks returns after a real JSON round-trip, not the writer's own return value — plus a near-miss guard that under-cap tasks stay byte-identical. It does not make a truncated replay faithful; the tail is gone. It only stops the loss being invisible.

What I didn't fix: h1's own named defect. Recording pr needs a schema field, a signature change, defensive parsing for old manifests, and the replay wiring — bigger than the round could land without risking reverting the whole thing. Filed as #815 rather than parked in prose, per the Day-164 rule.

